{
  "title": "Contract testing is not optional",
  "content": "The conventional advice on outbox pattern is incomplete in one specific, costly way. The workaround gets copy-pasted forward long after the original reason is forgotten. Here's what holds up in practice, and why.\n\nMost integration breakage I see in service meshes doesn't come from network partitions or bad deploys. It comes from two teams agreeing on a JSON shape in a Slack thread, then one team quietly adding a field, renaming another, and shipping. The consumer breaks three days later in a part of the system nobody was watching. Contract testing is the discipline that stops this, and I think it's the single highest-leverage practice a small platform team can adopt — higher than a service mesh, higher than a staging environment that mirrors production, higher than another round of end-to-end tests that take forty minutes to tell you nothing useful.\n\nThe problem this post solves: you have multiple services, you've felt the sting of a consumer breaking after a provider deploy, and you want to know whether contract testing is worth the setup cost — and if so, how to do it without turning your CI into a second job.\n\n## The conventional wisdom, and where it stops short\n\nThe standard advice is: write end-to-end tests. Spin up the whole stack in staging, hit the API, assert the response. If it passes, you're integrated. This is the advice you'll get from most tutorials, most conference talks, and most people who have never had to maintain a staging environment with eleven services and a database seeded by a script that only one person understands.\n\nThe second piece of conventional advice is: version your APIs. Use `/v2/`, deprecate `/v1/` on a schedule, publish a changelog. This is better than nothing. But versioning is a coordination mechanism, not a verification mechanism. It tells consumers that something *might* have changed. It doesn't tell you whether the consumer's assumptions still hold. A provider can add a required field to `/v2/` and break every consumer without ever touching `/v1/`. Versioning gives you a false sense of safety because it looks like a contract but isn't one — it's a label.\n\nThe third piece is: use a schema registry or OpenAPI spec and validate against it. This gets closer. But a static spec checked at build time only proves the provider *intends* to return a shape. It doesn't prove the consumer *actually depends on* that shape. If the provider removes a field the consumer never used, the spec check fails and you've blocked a harmless change. If the provider changes a field's semantics without changing its type — say, `amount` goes from cents to dollars — the spec check passes and the consumer silently computes wrong totals for a week.\n\nSo the conventional wisdom is incomplete in a specific way: it treats integration as a property of the *provider* when it's actually a property of the *relationship* between provider and consumer. Contract testing is the only practice I know that makes that relationship explicit and testable.\n\n## What actually happens when you follow the standard advice\n\nThe typical failure mode with end-to-end-only testing looks like this. Team A owns the orders service. Team B owns the notifications service. Notifications calls orders to get order details before sending a receipt. The end-to-end test in staging passes because staging has a version of orders that returns `{ id, total, currency, customer_email }`. Team A ships a change that renames `customer_email` to `email` because a new frontend wants it that way. The end-to-end test in staging still passes — because staging's notifications service is running an older build that hasn't been redeployed, and the test fixture mocks the orders response anyway. Production breaks. The receipt emails go out with `undefined` in the greeting.\n\nThis isn't a hypothetical. It's the most common shape of integration bug I see described in postmortems: the test that was supposed to catch it was testing a *copy* of the integration, not the integration itself. Staging is a copy. Mocks are copies. The only thing that isn't a copy is the consumer's actual expectations, encoded somewhere the provider's CI can run against.\n\nA second failure mode: the end-to-end suite becomes so slow and flaky that teams stop trusting it. It takes thirty minutes, fails two out of five runs for unrelated reasons, and gets retried until green. At that point it's not a test, it's a ritual. Teams start merging on red because \"it's probably the flaky one.\" The integration breakage ships anyway, and now you've also lost the signal that would have told you.\n\nA third: the provider team doesn't know who its consumers are. They ship a change, something breaks, and the incident channel fills with people from a team they've never heard of. The provider had no way to run the consumers' checks because the consumers never published them anywhere the provider could reach.\n\n## A different mental model: the contract is a consumer-owned artifact\n\nThe shift that makes contract testing work is this: **the contract belongs to the consumer, not the provider.** The provider publishes an API. The consumer writes down the subset of that API it actually depends on — the fields it reads, the status codes it handles, the headers it requires — and that written-down subset becomes a test the provider must pass before shipping.\n\nThis inverts the usual direction. Instead of the provider publishing a spec and hoping consumers conform, consumers publish their expectations and the provider verifies against them. The provider's CI runs every consumer's contract. If a change would break a consumer, the provider's build goes red *before* the change ships, and the provider team can see exactly which consumer and which field.\n\nThis is the model popularized by consumer-driven contract testing tools. The best-known is Pact, which is open source and widely documented; Pact Broker is the piece that stores contracts and lets providers verify against them. I'm naming Pact because I'm confident it exists and works this way, but the model is tool-agnostic. You can implement a crude version with JSON fixtures committed to a shared repo and a CI job that diffs them. The discipline matters more than the tool.\n\nThe key properties of this model:\n\n1. **Contracts are narrow.** A consumer only records what it uses. If it reads `id` and `total` and ignores `currency`, the contract doesn't mention `currency`. This means the provider can freely change fields nobody depends on — which is the whole point. Broad contracts (\"the response matches this full schema\") recreate the problem you were trying to solve.\n\n2. **Contracts are versioned alongside consumer code.** When a consumer stops using a field, the contract updates in the same PR. When a consumer starts using a new field, the contract updates and the provider's next build verifies it. The contract is not a separate document that drifts; it's a test artifact.\n\n3. **Verification runs in the provider's CI, not a shared staging environment.** This is what makes it fast and reliable. No spin-up, no seeding, no flaky network. The provider's test suite replays each consumer's recorded expectations against the provider's actual code.\n\n4. **Breaking changes are visible before merge.** The provider team sees \"this change breaks notifications-service contract v3\" in the PR, not in a production incident three days later.\n\n## Documented behavior and typical examples\n\nPact's documentation describes the flow clearly: consumers generate pact files (JSON documents describing requests and expected responses), publish them to a broker, and providers run a verification step that replays those requests against the real provider and asserts the responses match. The broker tracks which provider versions have verified which consumer versions, so you can answer \"is this provider version safe to deploy?\" by checking that all active consumer contracts pass.\n\nA minimal consumer-side contract in Pact's JavaScript DSL looks roughly like this (the exact API varies by language and version; check the docs for your binding):\n\n```javascript\nconst { PactV3, MatchersV3 } = require('@pact-foundation/pact');\nconst { like, integer } = MatchersV3;\n\nconst provider = new PactV3({\n  consumer: 'notifications-service',\n  provider: 'orders-service',\n});\n\ndescribe('orders contract', () => {\n  it('returns order details for a receipt', async () => {\n    provider\n      .given('order 123 exists')\n      .uponReceiving('a request for order 123')\n      .withRequest({ method: 'GET', path: '/orders/123' })\n      .willRespondWith({\n        status: 200,\n        body: {\n          id: like('123'),\n          total: integer(4500),\n          email: like('a@b.com'),\n        },\n      });\n\n    await provider.executeTest(async (mockServer) => {\n      const res = await fetch(`${mockServer.url}/orders/123`);\n      const body = await res.json();\n      expect(body.email).toBeDefined();\n      expect(body.total).toBeGreaterThan(0);\n    });\n  });\n});\n```\n\nThe `like` and `integer` matchers are the important part. They say \"this field exists and has this type,\" not \"this field equals this exact value.\" That's what lets the contract survive harmless provider changes. If the provider changes `total` from `4500` to `4600`, the contract still passes. If it changes `total` from an integer to a string, the contract fails — correctly.\n\nOn the provider side, verification is typically a test that loads each consumer's pact file and replays the recorded requests against the provider's running instance (often a test server, not a full deployment). The provider team wires this into CI as a required check. The failure output names the consumer and the mismatched field, which is what makes it actionable.\n\nA worked example of the decision this enables: suppose orders-service wants to remove the `email` field because a new `contact` object replaces it. The provider team opens a PR. CI runs the notifications-service contract, which expects `email`. The build fails. The provider team now has three options: keep `email` and add `contact` alongside it (backward compatible), coordinate with notifications-service to update its contract first (a two-step deploy), or accept the break and version the endpoint. Without contract testing, the same change would have shipped and broken receipts in production. The contract didn't make the change impossible; it made the *cost* of the change visible at the moment someone could still decide.\n\n## The cases where the conventional wisdom is right\n\nI'm not arguing contract testing replaces everything. There are cases where end-to-end tests and staging environments are the right tool, and pretending otherwise would be dishonest.\n\n**UI flows and cross-service user journeys.** Contract testing verifies that service A's response matches what service B expects. It does not verify that a user can log in, add an item to a cart, and check out. For that you need an end-to-end test, and you should have a small number of them covering the critical paths. The mistake is having *only* end-to-end tests, or having hundreds of them because you don't trust the contracts.\n\n**Infrastructure and configuration.** Contract testing doesn't catch a missing environment variable, a misconfigured load balancer, or a database migration that didn't run. Those are deployment concerns and need their own checks — smoke tests post-deploy, health checks, canary analysis.\n\n**Third-party APIs you don't control.** If you're consuming Stripe or a government payment gateway, you can't make them verify your contract. You can write consumer-side tests against their documented responses, but the provider won't run them. That's a real limitation. The mitigation is to pin to documented API versions and monitor response shapes in production.\n\n**Very small systems.** If you have two services and one team owns both, contract testing is overhead. A shared test suite that imports both services and asserts the integration is simpler and catches the same bugs. Contract testing earns its keep when ownership is split across teams and the provider can't easily run the consumer's code.\n\n**Event-driven and async systems.** Contract testing for message queues and event streams is possible — Pact supports message contracts — but it's more complex than request/response. If your integration is async, budget more time for setup and expect rougher edges.\n\n## How to decide whether it fits your situation\n\nThe decision framework I'd use:\n\n| Signal | Lean toward contract testing | Lean toward end-to-end only |\n|---|---|---|\n| Number of teams owning services | 2+ teams, separate ownership | 1 team owns both sides |\n| Frequency of provider changes | Weekly or more | Rarely |\n| Cost of a production break | High (money, trust, compliance) | Low (internal tool, easy rollback) |\n| Staging environment reliability | Flaky, slow, hard to seed | Fast, reliable, representative |\n| Consumer count per provider | 3+ consumers | 1 consumer |\n| Team familiarity with testing infra | Some CI experience | No CI beyond running tests |\n\nIf you check three or more in the left column, contract testing will pay for itself. If you check mostly right-column, start with a shared integration test suite and revisit when ownership splits.\n\nThe cost side, honestly: expect a few days of setup for the first provider-consumer pair — installing the tooling, wiring the broker (or a shared repo if you're doing the crude version), and getting the provider verification into CI as a required check. After that, adding a new consumer is mostly writing one contract file and publishing it. The ongoing cost is keeping contracts narrow; teams drift toward recording full responses because it's easier, and that drift recreates the brittleness you were trying to escape.\n\n## Common objections, and responses\n\n**\"Our API is internal, we don't need contracts.\"** Internal APIs are exactly where this matters most, because there's no external pressure to keep them stable. External APIs have customers who complain; internal APIs have consumers who silently break. The absence of a contract is why internal API changes are more dangerous, not less.\n\n**\"We use OpenAPI, isn't that a contract?\"** An OpenAPI spec describes what the provider *can* return. A contract describes what the consumer *requires*. These are different sets, and the gap between them is where breakage lives. You can generate consumer contracts from an OpenAPI spec as a starting point, but the consumer still has to narrow it to what it actually uses.\n\n**\"This is just more tests to maintain.\"** It is more tests, but they're tests that fail for one reason: a real integration break. They don't flake on network, they don't need a database, and they run in seconds. The maintenance cost is lower than the end-to-end suite they let you shrink.\n\n**\"Our provider team won't run consumer contracts.\"** Then the contracts won't work, and that's an organizational problem, not a tooling one. The honest answer is that contract testing requires the provider team to accept a CI check they don't control. If that's a non-starter, you're back to staging and hope. I'd argue the conversation about *why* the provider won't run a fast, deterministic check is more valuable than the tooling itself.\n\n**\"We tried Pact and it was too complex.\"** Pact's full setup (broker, versioning, webhooks) is genuinely a lot for a small team. The crude version — JSON fixtures in a shared repo, a CI job that replays them — captures most of the value with a fraction of the setup. Start crude, upgrade if you outgrow it.\n\n## What the alternative approach would change\n\nThe practical difference shows up in how teams talk about deploys. With end-to-end-only testing, the provider team's deploy conversation is \"did staging pass?\" — a question about a copy. With contract testing, it's \"did every consumer's contract verify?\" — a question about the actual relationships. The second question is answerable in the PR, by the person making the change, before merge.\n\nIt also changes what a breaking change costs. Today, a provider team that renames a field finds out from an incident. With contracts, they find out from a red CI check that names the consumer and the field, and they can decide whether to keep the old field, coordinate a migration, or accept the break. The change still might happen; it just stops being a surprise.\n\nAnd it changes the end-to-end suite. Once contracts cover the integration points, the end-to-end tests can shrink to the handful of user journeys that actually need a full stack. That's the outcome I'd argue for: contracts for the seams, end-to-end for the journeys, and staging for the things neither can catch.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**How is contract testing different from integration testing?** Integration testing typically spins up real services and tests them together, which is slow and environment-dependent. Contract testing records the consumer's expectations and replays them against the provider in isolation, so it runs fast and deterministically in CI. The tradeoff is that contracts only cover the request/response shapes, not the full behavior of the system.\n\n**Do I need a broker to do contract testing?** No. A broker (like Pact Broker) makes it easier to share contracts across teams and track which versions have verified, but a shared Git repository with JSON fixtures and a CI job that replays them captures most of the value for a small team. Start without a broker and add one when coordination overhead grows.\n\n**What happens when a consumer's contract is wrong?** The provider verification fails, and the consumer team needs to fix the contract or the provider needs to accommodate it. This is a feature, not a bug: a wrong contract is a wrong assumption about the API, and catching it before deploy is the point. The failure output should name the consumer so the right team gets pulled in.\n\n**Can contract testing catch breaking changes in event-driven systems?** Yes, but it's more involved. Pact supports message contracts, where the consumer records the message shape it expects and the provider verifies it can produce that shape. Async contracts are harder to reason about than request/response because ordering and delivery guarantees matter, so budget more setup time.\n\n## Summary\n\nContract testing is the discipline of making the consumer's expectations explicit and verifying them in the provider's CI. It doesn't replace end-to-end tests; it lets you shrink them to the journeys that need a full stack. It doesn't replace API versioning; it makes versioning decisions informed by actual consumer dependencies. The setup cost is real — a few days for the first pair — but the failure mode it prevents, a silent break in a service nobody was watching, is the most expensive kind of integration bug there is.\n\nYour next step, in the next thirty minutes: open the CI config for your most-changed provider service and check whether any job currently verifies a consumer's expectations. If not, pick one consumer, write a single JSON fixture describing the fields it reads from one endpoint, and add a CI step that asserts the provider's response includes those fields. That's a crude contract. It will catch the next rename before it ships.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
